<aside class="cart-drawer" data-cart-drawer aria-hidden="true" aria-label="Shopping bag">
  <div class="cart-drawer__backdrop" data-cart-close></div>
  <div class="cart-drawer__panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true">
    <div class="cart-drawer__head">
      <span class="h4">Your bag</span>
      <button class="icon-btn" data-cart-close aria-label="Close">{% render 'icon', name: 'close' %}</button>
    </div>

    <div class="cart-drawer__items" data-cart-items>
      {% if cart.item_count > 0 %}
        {% for item in cart.items %}
          <div class="cart-line" data-cart-line data-line-key="{{ item.key }}">
            <div class="cart-line__img"><img src="{{ item.image | image_url: width: 200 }}" alt="{{ item.product.title }}" width="200" height="200"></div>
            <div>
              <div class="cart-line__title">{{ item.product.title }}</div>
              {%- unless item.variant.title contains 'Default' -%}<div class="cart-line__variant">{{ item.variant.title }}</div>{%- endunless -%}
              {%- for prop in item.properties -%}
                {%- assign prop_prefix = prop.first | slice: 0 -%}
                {%- unless prop.last == blank or prop_prefix == '_' -%}
                  <div class="cart-line__prop">{{ prop.first }}: <strong>{{ prop.last }}</strong></div>
                {%- endunless -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
              {%- assign line_max = item.product.metafields.custom.max_per_order.value | default: 5 -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                An automatic discount that reaches only some of a line's
                units makes Shopify split it, so one product can hold two
                lines here. The cap is the PRODUCT's, so the quantity it
                is measured against is the product's across the cart, not
                this line's. theme.js reads the same sum off the steppers.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- assign product_total = 0 -%}
              {%- for other in cart.items -%}
                {%- if other.product_id == item.product_id -%}
                  {%- assign product_total = product_total | plus: other.quantity -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
              <div class="qty cart-drawer__qty" data-drawer-qty data-line-key="{{ item.key }}" data-product-id="{{ item.product_id }}" data-max="{{ line_max }}">
                <button type="button" data-drawer-qty-dec aria-label="Decrease quantity">&minus;</button>
                <input type="number" value="{{ item.quantity }}" min="0" max="{{ line_max }}" inputmode="numeric" data-drawer-qty-input aria-label="Quantity for {{ item.product.title | escape }}">
                <button type="button" data-drawer-qty-inc aria-label="Increase quantity">+</button>
              </div>
              {%- comment -%}
                One element, two jobs. It states the standing per-order limit
                when the line already sits at it, and the stepper writes into
                it when a press of + cannot be honoured - a stock ceiling or
                the cap - because this is the only place the shopper is
                looking at that moment. Rendered even when it has nothing to
                say so the stepper always has somewhere to write.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <p class="cart-line__limit" data-line-note data-line-key="{{ item.key }}" role="status"{% unless product_total >= line_max %} hidden{% endunless %}>
                {%- if product_total >= line_max -%}
                  {%- if line_max <= 3 -%}Shipping weight limits this to {{ line_max }} per order.
                  {%- else -%}Limited to {{ line_max }} per order.{%- endif -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              </p>
              <a href="#" data-cart-remove data-line-key="{{ item.key }}" class="cart-drawer__remove">Remove</a>
            </div>
            <div class="cart-line__price">{{ item.final_line_price | money }}</div>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      {% else %}
        <div class="cart-empty" style="text-align: center; padding: var(--space-7) var(--space-4);">
          <p class="muted">Your bag is empty.</p>
          <a href="{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}" class="btn btn-outline mt-3">Shop bestsellers</a>
        </div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% if cart.item_count > 0 %}
      <div class="cart-drawer__foot">
        <div class="cart-totals"><span>Subtotal</span><span>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</span></div>
        {%- comment -%}
          Shipping and gift progress used to be two separate blocks with two
          bars. They are one bar with two milestones now - see cart-gift.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {% render 'cart-gift' %}
        <a href="/checkout" class="btn btn-primary btn-block btn-lg">Checkout · {{ cart.total_price | money }}</a>
        <a href="{{ routes.cart_url }}" class="btn btn-ghost btn-block">View bag</a>
      </div>
    {% endif %}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Deliberately OUTSIDE the item_count guard above, and outside the panel.

    theme.js refreshes this drawer with drawer.innerHTML = newDrawer.innerHTML,
    and a <script> inserted through innerHTML never executes. So the gift
    picker's handler only ever runs on a real page load. While it lived inside
    cart-gift, a shopper who arrived with an empty cart never got it at all -
    the block had not rendered, so the tag was never in the document. They
    would add products, the drawer would refresh and show the chips, and
    clicking one did nothing until they reloaded the page. Rendering the assets
    unconditionally means the delegated listener is registered once at load and
    survives every later swap.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'cart-gift-assets' %}
</aside>
